.TH CCOL_CIRCQ_ENABLE_SENDING 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_circq_enable_sending \- turn on sends on a circular queue
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_circq_enable_sending(ccol_circular_queue *cq);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function clears the flag that ccol_circq_disable_sending() set, so
the queue accepts a send again. It also wakes every thread that blocks
and waits to send; a woken sender continues as soon as the queue has
room for its message.

A new queue has sends turned on from the start, so you need this call
only to open a queue again after a call turned sends off. A call on a
queue that already has sends on is harmless.

The function also wakes every write-direction waiter on this queue,
which is a ccol_select() call, a ccol_select_timed() call, or an
ccol_event_loop registration. Each of them scans again and sees the
new state.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cq   Circular queue that gets sends turned on
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success       - The function turned sends on
ccol_invalid_args  - cq is NULL
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function wakes every blocked sender with a broadcast, so a thread
can try to send immediately if the queue has free space.
The function does not change a receive.
The function is thread-safe.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_circq_disable_sending (3),
.BR ccol_circq_send_zc (3),
.BR ccol_circq_try_send_zc (3),
.BR ccol_circq_timed_send_zc (3)
